{"layer": "end_to_end", "dataset_id": "m11-heldout", "dataset_version": "m11-heldout-v1", "independence": "HELD_OUT", "case": "h-conflict-label-mismatch", "category": "known limitation: disagreeing values phrased differently", "question": "What illustrative infusion rate does this corpus give?", "answerable": false, "expected_gate": "SUFFICIENT", "actual_gate": "SUFFICIENT", "gate_agreed": true, "expected_outcome": "UNVERIFIED", "actual_outcome": "UNVERIFIED", "outcome_agreed": true, "verified": false, "answer": null, "verification_outcome": "ABSTAIN", "repair_count": 0, "expected_repair": false, "material_claims": 1, "unsupported_claims": 1, "reason_codes": ["SEMANTICALLY_UNSUPPORTED", "CONTRADICTED_BY_RETAINED_EVIDENCE", "AUTHORITATIVE_SOURCES_DISAGREE"], "expected_reason_codes": [], "reason_codes_matched": true, "unknown_reason_codes": [], "attributed_layer": "SUFFICIENCY", "expected_layer": "SUFFICIENCY", "layer_agreed": true, "note": "MEASURED LIMITATION, NOT A TARGET. The M7 conflict detector compares values only when the four-word label window preceding the number matches between sources. These two sources state different values for the same fictional quantity but phrase it differently, so the gate does not see a conflict and returns SUFFICIENT. The abstention here comes from M8 refusing the draft, not from M7 detecting the disagreement, and M8 reports the disagreement with the same AUTHORITATIVE_SOURCES_DISAGREE code the gate would have used. Attribution therefore points at SUFFICIENCY, which is the useful reading: the gate is where this should have been caught. The expectation records what the system actually does, so the gap is visible in every report rather than discovered later."}
